einrich M. Jaeger】 and 【15†Sidney R. Nagel】\nL68: \nL69:   * ^{}James Franck Institute, The University of Chicago, 5640 S. Ellis Avenue, Chicago, Illinois 60637\nL70: \nL71: 【16†Robert P. Behringer】\nL72: \nL73:   * ^{}Department of Physics, Duke University, Durham, North Carolina 27706\nL74: \nL75: 【17†PDF】\nL76: \nL77: Rev. Mod. Phys. 68, 1259 – Published 1 October, 1996\nL78: \nL79: DOI: https://doi.org/10.1103/RevModPhys.68.1259\nL80: [Button: Export Citation]\nL81: \nL82: Show metrics\nL83: ## Abstract\nL84: \nL85: Granular materials are ubiquitous in the world around us. They have properties that are different from those commonly associated with either solids, liquids, or gases. In this review the authors select some of the special properties of granular materials and describe recent research developments.[S0034-6861(96)00204-8]\nL86: \nL87: [Button: Close]\nL88: \nL89: [Button: Prev][Button: Next]\nL90: \nL91: ##\nL92: \nL93: 【18†View figure in article】\nL94: # Authorization Required\nL95: \nL96: We need you to provide your credentials before accessing this content.\nL97: \nL98: ## Log in via your institution\nL99: \nL100: If your institution provides access using Shibboleth/OpenAthens log in.\nL101: \nL102: ## Log in via APS Member Subscription\nL103: \nL104: If you have a personal subscription through your APS membership please log in.\nL105: \nL106: Starting August 1, 2019 APS member subscribers will need to log in using your member credentials instead of your APS Journal Account.\nL107: \nL108: 【19†APS Member Log In】\nL109: ## Other Options\nL110: \nL111:   * 【20†Buy Article »】\nL112:   * 【21†Log in with APS Journals Account】\nL113:   * 【22†Log in with username/password provided by your institution】\nL114:   * 【23†Get access through a U.S. public or high school】\nL115: ## 【17†References (Subscription Required)】\nL116: \nL117: [Button: Outline][Button: Information]\nL118: \nL119:   * 【24†Citing Articles (2527)】\nL120:   * 【25†Abstract】\nL121:   * 【26†Refer