ov】\nL16: \nL17: Search [Input: Search NCBI]\nL18: \nL19: Logged in as:\nL20:   * 【5†Dashboard†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL21:   * 【6†Publications†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL22:   * 【7†Account settings†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL23: \nL24: [Button: Log in ]\nL25: \nL26: 【1†Image: PMC search open icon†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】 【8†Image: PMC search close ison†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL27: \nL28: Search PMC Full-Text Archive [Input: Search PMC Full-Text Archive] Search in PMC 【9†Image: Search†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL29: \nL30:   * 【10†Journal List】\nL31:   * 【11†User Guide】\nL32: \nL33: [Button: Biochemistry]\nL34: \nL35: . Author manuscript; available in PMC: 2012 Oct 4.\nL36: Published in final edited form as: Biochemistry. 2011 Sep 9;50(39):8264–8269. doi: 【12†10.1021/bi201284u†doi.org】\nL37: # The Original Michaelis Constant: Translation of the 1913 Michaelis-Menten Paper\nL38: \nL39: 【13†Kenneth A Johnson†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL40: \nL41: ### Kenneth A Johnson\nL42: \nL43: ^{1}Professor of Biochemistry, Institute for Cell and Molecular Biology, 2500 Speedway, University of Texas, Austin, TX 78735 USA\nL44: \nL45: Find articles by 【13†Kenneth A Johnson†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL46: \nL47: ^{1}, 【14†Roger S Goody†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL48: ### Roger S Goody\nL49: \nL50: ^{2}Director of the Dept. of Physical Biochemistry, Max-Planck Institute of Molecular Physiology, Otto-Hahn-Strasse 11, 44227 Dortmund, Germany\nL51: \nL52: Find articles by 【14†Roger S Goody†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL53: \nL54: ^{2}\nL55: \nL56:   * [Button: Author information]\nL57:   * [Button: Article notes]\nL58:   * [Button: Copyright and License information]\nL59: \nL60: ^{1}Professor of Biochemistry, Institute for Cell and Molecular Biology, 2500 Speedway, University of Texas, Austin, TX 78735 USA\nL61: ^{2}Director of the Dept. of Physical Biochemistry, Max-Planck Institute of Molecular Physiology, Otto-Hahn-Strasse 11, 44227 Dortmund, Germany\nL62: \nL63: ^{✉}\nL64: \nL65: Corresponding Author: Kenneth A. Johnson, Department of Chemistry & Biochemistry, Institute for Cellular and Molecular Biology, The University of Texas, Austin, Texas 78712, kajohnson@mail.utexas.edu, Phone: 512-471-0434, Fax: 512-471-0435\nL66: \nL67: Issue date 2011 Oct 4.\nL68: \nL69: 【15†PMC Copyright notice】\nL70: PMCID: PMC3381512  NIHMSID: NIHMS324930  PMID: 【16†21888353†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL71: ## Abstract\nL72: Nearly 100 years ago Michaelis and Menten published their now classic paper (Michaelis, L., and Menten, M. L. (1913) Die Kinetik der Invertinwirkung, Biochemische Zeitschrift 49, 333–369), in which they show that the rate of an enzyme-catalyzed reaction is proportional to the concentration of enzyme-substrate complex predicted by the Michaelis-Menten equation.\nL73: Because the original text was written in German, yet is often quoted by English speaking authors, we undertook a complete translation of the 1913 publication, which we provide as an 【17†online supplement】 (【18†http://pubs.acs.org†pubs.acs.org】). Here we introduce the translation, describe the historical context of the work, and show a new analysis of the original data. In doing so, we uncovered several surprises that reveal an interesting glimpse into the early history of enzymology.\nL74: In particular, our re-analysis of Michaelis and Menten’s data using modern computational methods revealed an unanticipated rigor and precision in the original publication and uncovered a sophisticated, comprehensive analysis that has been overlooked in the century since their work was published.\nL75: Michaelis and Menten not only analyzed initial velocity measurements, but they also fit their full time course data to the integrated form of the rate equations, including product inhibition, and derived a single global constant to represent all of their data. That constant was not the Michaelis constant, but rather, V_{max}/K_{m}, the specificity constant times the enzyme concentration (k_{cat}/K_{m}*E_{0}).\nL76: * * *\nL77: \nL78: In 1913 Leonor Michaelis and Maud Leonora Menten published their now classic paper, Die Kinetik der Invertinwerkung (【19†1】). They studied invertase, which was so named because its reaction results in the inversion of optical rotation from positive for sucrose to a net negative for the sum of fructose plus glucose.\nL79: \nL80: $$\\text{Sucrose}\\stackrel{\\mathit{Invertase}}{\\to }\\text{Fructose}+\\text{Glucose}$$\nL81: After receiving her M.D. degree in 1911 at the University of Toronto, Maud L. Menten (1879 – 1960) worked as a research assistant in the Berlin laboratory of Leonor Michaelis (1875–1949). She monitored the rate of the invertase-catalyzed reaction at several sucrose concentrations by careful measurement of optical rotation as a function of time, following the reaction to completion.\nL82: Their goal was to test the theory that “invertase forms a complex with sucrose that is very labile and decays to free enzyme, glucose and fructose” leading to the prediction that “the rate of inversion must be proportional to the prevailing concentration of sucrose-enzyme complex.” Michaelis and Menten recognized that the products of the reaction were inhibitory, as known from prior work by Henri (【20†2】).\n"